t 1 likely contains the full description, I could keep certified_type minimal here. But better to include what's derivable: category \"Water meter\", type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]. Wait, careful: \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\" — the type designation FEW45Y.Z is a Sensus AquaMaster4 type. Include type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]. Since other fragments (1,3,4) may have more designations, but I only handle this fragment.\n\nHmm, actually since the instruction says extract content present in THIS fragment only, and each fragment includes the certificate header (number, project, pages), I should include certificate fields. Good.\n\nAlso \"AquaMaster4\" as family_name: yes.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\"? The certificate number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" → recommendation R49, edition 2013. The scheme letter appears between: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01. So recommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\"}. accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — from tables (accuracy class 1 and 2). Include: \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2].\n\nR49-1:2013 is referenced: \"paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013\" — edition 2013 confirmed.\n\ntest_reports: none in fragment. Omit.\n\nrevision_history: include {\"revision\": \"11\"}? The header says \"revision 11\". I'll include revision_history: [{\"revision\": \"11\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}]? Hmm, \"Don't fabricate... Empty strings are not values — use null or omit.\" I'll include with date/changes omitted (null). Actually maybe cleaner: omit revision_history and put number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" plus add \"revision\": \"11\"? Not in schema. I'll go with revision_history minimal entry. Hmm... Actually, thinking again: revision_history implies the certificate's revision history; fragment only shows current revision number. Including {\"revision\": \"11\"} captures that. I'll do it.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Scheme: \"A\" (from number pattern). page_total: 13. project_number: \"3891281\" (string or number? \"3891281\" — I'll use string \"3891281\"; number is fine too. Project numbers can have leading zeros conceptually; string safer).\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\". website/email not present. person_responsible not present. Should I include member_state? It's under certificate. Include issuing_authority.name \"NMi\". NMi's full name \"NMi Certin B.V.\" not in fragment — don't fabricate.\n\nNow, the general characteristics notes (bullets) — capture as type_level characteristics or description? I'll add to characteristics.type_level:\n- q2_q1_ratio: {value: 1.6} (with note that Q1 and Q2 values are minimum values) \n- q4_q3_ratio: {value: 1.25}\n- q3_q1_ratio: hmm this varies per model — as a constraint \"at least 40\" → minimum_ratio_q3_q1: {value: {min: 40, max: null}}. \n\nBut careful: type_level single value — q2_q1_ratio 1.6 is a fixed requirement (both tables). Yes bullets identical in both pages. q4_q3_ratio 1.25 fixed. Include these three.\n\nAlso \"Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013\" — context; skip or put in description. I'll keep matrix table descriptions mentioning freedom of choice briefly.\n\nNow let me think about models list more concretely:\n\nmodels: [\n  {\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"label\": \"DN40 reduced bore\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_classes\": [1,2], \"power_supply\": [\"AC mains\", \"Battery / Renewable energy\"]}},\n  ... for each size\n]\n\nHmm — attributes with lists; fine. Or simpler: {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}. The schema allows attributes{}. I'll include attributes with accuracy_classes and power_supply_options. Wait \"Renewable energy\" — the header says \"Battery / Renewable energy powered\". I'll use \"Battery / Renewable energy\".\n\nDo all sizes support both classes? DN65 AC \"1 & 2\" yes; battery separate 1 and 2. All sizes have both classes. OK attributes: accuracy_classes [1,2] for all. That's uniform... then maybe skip per-model attributes and just have model_id list. But then the \"1&2\" info is in tables anyway. I'll include minimal attributes: {\"power_supply_options\": [\"AC mains\", \"Battery / Renewable energy\"]} for each — uniform, redundant. Maybe skip attributes entirely and keep models simple: {\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"label\": \"DN40\"}. Hmm, model label equal to id is fine. I'll just do {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"} etc.\n\nWait, actually — should models be size-only or size+class? Let me reconsider once more. The natural \"model variant\" for a water meter family is the size (DN) with nominal flow. The class is a rating. I'll do size-only models and put q3/q4 in model_level, q1/q2/ratio in config_level with compound conditions (size + class + power) — axis \"power_supply\" doesn't work with compound... I'll use axis \"accuracy_class\" with conditions \"DN40 AC mains class 1\" — awkward.\n\nAlternative cleaner: config_level axis = \"power_supply\"; and model_level includes class-specific attributes with model ids \"DN40 class 1\"? Mixed model id schemes within model_family.models (size-only) vs model_level model refs (size+class) — inconsistent.\n\nYou know what, let me just make models = size+class combos WITH power as a config axis at model_level? No — flow rates differ by power, so class+size doesn't determine flow either.\n\nFinal call: I'll make model_level entries for q3 and q4 (size-determined), and put q1/q2/ratio ONLY in matrix_tables (which contain all data). config_level: skip — it doesn't fit the 3-dim structure, and matrix_tables explicitly exist for this. Hmm, but rule 5 gives config_level for config axes like \"static vs dynamic\" — 2 conditions. Our power axis has 2 conditions but each condition still varies by model AND class. If I wanted, I could do TWO model_level sets: attributes suffixed by power? Like \"minimum_flow_rate_q1_ac_mains\" and \"minimum_flow_rate_q1_battery\" with model ids \"DN40 class 1\"? That's hacky but structured:\n\nmodel_level:\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", unit \"m³/h\", values per model where model = \"DN40 AC class 1\"... \n\nI'll go with model ids encoding all: Actually, let me just count: I could define models as (size, class, power) = e.g. \"DN40|1|AC\" — 50 models. Then model_level attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio each with 50 values. Fully normalized, machine-friendly. It's verbose but complete and unambiguous. The matrix_tables would then be redundant but still included for fidelity.\n\nOutput size estimate: 50 models × ~15 tokens = 750 tokens; model_level 5 × 50 values × ~12 tokens = 3000 tokens; matrix rows 50 × ~25 tokens = 1250 tokens. Total maybe 5-6k tokens. That's large but acceptable? The answer would be huge. Given \"Return only the JSON\", a 6k-token JSON is risky for truncation. Let me find the middle ground:\n\nMiddle ground (my choice):\n- matrix_tables: 2 flow tables (complete, 24 + 26 rows) — full fidelity.\n- 3 software matrix_tables (2 single-part-code tables + Table 4).\n- model_family: family_name \"AquaMaster4\", models = 15 sizes.\n- model_level: q3, q4 per size (15 values each).\n- config_level: q1, q2, ratio with axis \"power_supply\" and conditions like \"AC mains — DN40, class 1\"? Hmm, still 50 entries per attribute.\n\nNo. Skip config_level. Matrix tables carry q1/q2/ratio. Done deliberating.\n\nHmm wait, actually, one more idea: model_level with model = \"DN40\" and values per accuracy class for ratio... ratio varies by class AND power. No.\n\nOK final structure. Let me also reconsider whether model_level for q3/q4 with size-only models is consistent: yes, q3/q4 constant across class and power, verified. Good — this demonstrates matrix detection properly.\n\nType-level characteristics from bullets:\n- \"q2_q1_ratio\": {value: 1.6} — label \"q2_q1_ratio\". \n- \"q4_q3_ratio\": {value: 1.25}\n- \"minimum_q3_q1_ratio\": {value: {min: 40, max: null}} — \"The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40\". unit null.\n\nMaybe also note q1/q2 are minimums and q3/q4 maximums — could be footnote-ish; I'll fold into matrix table description text.\n\nNow build the JSON.\n\ncertificate:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"3891281\",\n  \"page_total\": 13,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n}\n\nrevision_history: [{\"revision\": \"11\"}]. Hmm, do I include? I'll include: {\"revision\": \"11\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}. Actually including nulls is noise; schema shows fields revision, date, changes. I'll write {\"revision\": \"11\", \"date\": null, \"changes\": null}. Fine.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe better to not include revision_history because \"revision 11\" is just the current state, and rule 8 says omit what's not present. But revision 11 IS present. I'll include it.\n\nissuing_authority:\n{\"name\": \"NMi\", \"address_lines\": [], \"oiml_issuer_id\": null} — omit empty arrays/nulls? Rule: omit fields not present. I'll include just name. Maybe member_state belongs to certificate only. OK: {\"name\": \"NMi\"}.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]\n}\nHmm, is \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" a type designation? Table 4: \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\". I'll record type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]. Good.\n\nShould category be \"Water meter\"? I'll include — R49 = water meters. Yes.\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"AquaMaster4\",\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN50\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN65\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN80\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN100\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN125\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN150\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN200\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN250\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN300\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN350\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN400\"},\n    {\"model_id\": \"DN450\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN500\"}, {\"model_id\": \"DN600\"}\n  ]\n}\n\nMaybe add attributes to each: {\"accuracy_classes\": [1,2]} — since all uniform, and \"power_supply_options\": [...]. I'll include attributes per model:\n{\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2], \"power_supply\": [\"AC mains\", \"Battery / Renewable energy\"]}}\nUniform for all 15. This adds info clearly. OK.\n\ncomponents: skip? Or include MCU software? I'll skip. Hmm — actually, maybe include software part codes as components? \"Software part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\" — these are firmware configurations. They could be components with role \"software\" and characteristics... The schema components characteristics{} is free-form. But matrix_tables already handle them. Skip components to avoid duplication.\n\ncharacteristics:\ntype_level: {\n  \"q2_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"q4_q3_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.25, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"minimum_q3_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 40, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n}\n\nWait — labels canonical snake_case. \"q2_q1_ratio\" fine. Hmm \"ratio q2/q1 = 1,6\" → 1.6. \"ratio q4/q3 = 1,25\" → 1.25. Good.\n\nmodel_level: [\n  {\"attribute\": \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 25}, {\"model\": \"DN50\", \"value\": 40}, {\"model\": \"DN65\", \"value\": 63},\n    {\"model\": \"DN80\", \"value\": 100}, {\"model\": \"DN100\", \"value\": 160}, {\"model\": \"DN125\", \"value\": 160},\n    {\"model\": \"DN150\", \"value\": 400}, {\"model\": \"DN200\", \"value\": 630}, {\"model\": \"DN250\", \"value\": 1000},\n    {\"model\": \"DN300\", \"value\": 1600}, {\"model\": \"DN350\", \"value\": 1600}, {\"model\": \"DN400\", \"value\": 2500},\n    {\"model\": \"DN450\", \"value\": 2500}, {\"model\": \"DN500\", \"value\": 4000}, {\"model\": \"DN600\", \"value\": 6300}\n  ]},\n  {\"attribute\": \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [\n    {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 31.25}, {\"model\": \"DN50\", \"value\": 50}, {\"model\": \"DN65\", \"value\": 78.75},\n    {\"model\": \"DN80\", \"value\": 125}, {\"model\": \"DN100\", \"value\": 200}, {\"model\": \"DN125\", \"value\": 200},\n    {\"model\": \"DN150\", \"value\": 500}, {\"model\": \"DN200\", \"value\": 787.5}, {\"model\": \"DN250\", \"value\": 1250},\n    {\"model\": \"DN300\", \"value\": 2000}, {\"model\": \"DN350\", \"value\": 2000}, {\"model\": \"DN400\", \"value\": 3125},\n    {\"model\": \"DN450\", \"value\": 3125}, {\"model\": \"DN500\", \"value\": 5000}, {\"model\": \"DN600\", \"value\": 7875}\n  ]}\n]\n\nWait — should I double-check Q3 for DN125: AC table DN125 Q3=160, Q4=200 ✓ battery same ✓. DN100: 160/200 ✓.\n\nHmm, also should model_level include unit_id? Structured form includes unit_id: null unless certain. m³/h → unitsdb likely \"u:cubic_metre_per_hour\" but rule says leave null unless certain. I'll leave unit_id out or null. The shape example includes unit_id in value objects: {\"value\", \"unit_symbol\", \"unit_id\", \"footnote_markers\"}. The R137 example model_level entries omit unit_id (\"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}]) and have attribute-level unit_symbol. I'll follow the R137 example: attribute-level unit_symbol, values with model/value. Simpler and sanctioned by example. But for type_level, use the full structured form with unit_id: null.\n\nconfig_level: []? Omit (don't include empty). Or include q1/q2/ratio? I decided matrix_tables only. Hmm, wait. Let me reconsider one more time... The \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule says varying attributes → model_level, not type_level. Q1/Q2/ratio vary by model too (per size). If I only put them in matrix_tables, a downstream consumer relying on model_level would miss them. The R137 example: matrix has more columns (cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, pmax) but model_level only got qmax/qt/qmin — so it's OK for matrix_tables to carry more than model_level! Great — precedent supports putting q1/q2/ratio in matrix_tables only. And q3/q4 I'll ALSO have in model_level (they're size-only). Actually the R137 example also left cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, pmax in the matrix only even though they're size-only constants... whatever. I'll include q3/q4 in model_level as bonus normalization. Fine.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n\n1. Software table for part code 3KXF208402U0113:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software identification — part code 3KXF208402U0113 (Full functionality)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"remarks\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.00.03\", \"crc_checksum\": \"B26AFE6B\", \"remarks\": null},\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.00.04\", \"crc_checksum\": \"6E98963A\", \"remarks\": null},\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.02.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"E12E5C04\", \"remarks\": null},\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.03.02\", \"crc_checksum\": \"D4D1E40B\", \"remarks\": \"HMI display bug fixes. Low flow output bug fix.\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.03.03\", \"crc_checksum\": \"CEEF45F1\", \"remarks\": \"Modbus communication bug fix.\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.03.06\", \"crc_checksum\": \"DAC51328\", \"remarks\": null},\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.04.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"277BA336\", \"remarks\": null},\n    {\"software_version\": \"03.04.02\", \"crc_checksum\": \"66624063\", \"remarks\": null}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Meter software versions and CRC checksums, full functionality software part.\"\n}\n\n2. Part code 3KXF208402U0313 (Sensus / Logger / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\nrows: {\"02.00.02\", \"84EBD1C8\"}, {\"02.00.04\", \"5D2D2DDD\"}.\n\n3. Part code 3KXF208402U0513 (Modbus / Pressure / VKL sensor updater):\nrows: {\"02.00.01\", \"C28EAA16\"}, {\"02.00.05\", \"F517559F\"}.\n\n4. Table 4 — AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software identification for AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z variant\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_function\", \"mcu\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, null, null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_function\": \"Measurement\", \"mcu\": \"Measurement MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.02.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"94E6FAE9\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"Measurement\", \"mcu\": \"Measurement MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"A6C913AF\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"Measurement\", \"mcu\": \"Measurement MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.01\", \"crc_checksum\": \"2BB4AE79\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"4G\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.02.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"AE3D9862\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"4G\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"3B33D2C8\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"4G\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.01\", \"crc_checksum\": \"FDE7CBB6\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"4G\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.02\", \"crc_checksum\": \"CD0C1881\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"NB-IoT\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.00\", \"crc_checksum\": \"E5742163\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"NB-IoT\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.01\", \"crc_checksum\": \"EC4FF9C6\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"NB-IoT\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.02\", \"crc_checksum\": \"C8CABC2D\"},\n    {\"software_function\": \"NB-IoT\", \"mcu\": \"Communication MCU\", \"software_version\": \"01.03.03\", \"crc_checksum\": \"31459473\"}\n  ],\n  \"footnotes\": [\"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"]\n}\n\nShould the footnote be in global footnotes[] too? Schema has global footnotes[]. I'll add global footnotes entry:\n{\"marker\": \"1\", \"text\": \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"}\nMarker: the markdown shows [1]. Examples use \"1)\". I'll use \"[1]\" as it appears? Rule 7 example: footnotes[] with marker \"1)\". I'll use \"1\".\n\n5. Table 5 — AC mains flow characteristics:\n{\n  \"name\": \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — Reduced bore, AC mains powered (Table 5)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"minimum_flow_q1\", \"transitional_flow_q2\", \"permanent_flow_q3\", \"overload_flow_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"minimum_flow_q1\": 0.05, \"transitional_flow_q2\": 0.08, \"permanent_flow_q3\": 25, \"overload_flow_q4\": 31.25, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN40\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, \"minimum_flow_q1\": 0.025, \"transitional_flow_q2\": 0.04, \"permanent_flow_q3\": 25, \"overload_flow_q4\": 31.25, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN50\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, \"minimum_flow_q1\": 0.08, \"transitional_flow_q2\": 0.128, \"permanent_flow_q3\": 40, \"overload_flow_q4\": 50, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN50\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, \"minimum_flow_q1\": 0.04, \"transitional_flow_q2\": 0.064, \"permanent_flow_q3\": 40, \"overload_flow_q4\": 50, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN65\", \"accuracy_class\": [1, 2], \"minimum_flow_q1\": 0.063, \"transitional_flow_q2\": 0.1008, \"permanent_flow_q3\": 63, \"overload_flow_q4\": 78.75, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN80\", \"accuracy_class\": [1, 2], \"minimum_flow_q1\": 0.1, \"transitional_flow_q2\": 0.16, \"permanent_flow_q3\": 100, \"overload_flow_q4\": 125, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN100\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, ... 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN100\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, ... 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN125\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN125\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN150\", \"accuracy_class\": [1,2], 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN200\", \"accuracy_class\": [1,2], 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN250\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN250\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN300\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN300\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN350\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN350\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN400\", \"accuracy_class\": [1,2], 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN450\", \"accuracy_class\": [1,2], 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN500\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN500\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN600\", \"accuracy_class\": 1, 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN600\", \"accuracy_class\": 2, 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Q1 and Q2 are minimum values (Q2/Q1 = 1.6); Q3 and Q4 are maximum values (Q4/Q3 = 1.25); ratio Q3/Q1 at least 40. Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013.\",\n}\n\n6. Table 6 — battery:\nrows as transcribed above, 26 rows.\n\nCheck battery DN65 class1 Q1: \"0,39375\" → 0.39375. Q2 \"0,63\" → 0.63. Ratio 160. Class 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 400. Ratio check: Q3/Q1: 63/0.39375 = 160 ✓; 63/0.1575 = 400 ✓. Good consistency.\n\nDN80 battery: c1 0.625 (100/0.625=160 ✓), 1; c2 0.25 (400 ✓), 0.4.\nDN100: c1 1 (160 ✓) 1.6; c2 0.4 (400 ✓) 0.64.\nDN150: 1&2: Q1 1 (400/1=400 ✓) Q2 1.6.\nDN200: 1.575 → 630/1.575 = 400 ✓; Q2 2.52.\nDN250: c1 6.25 (1000/6.25=160 ✓), 10; c2 2.5 (400 ✓), 4.\nDN300: c1 10 (160 ✓), 16; c2 4 (400 ✓), 6.4.\nDN350: same as 300.\nDN400: c1 15.625 (2500/15.625=160 ✓), 25; c2 12.5 (200 ✓), 20.\nDN450: same.\nDN500: c1 100 (40 ✓), 160; c2 40 (100 ✓), 64.\nDN600: c1 157.5 (40 ✓), 252; c2 63 (100 ✓), 100.8.\n\nAC checks: DN65: 63/0.063 = 1000 ✓. DN150: 400/0.4 = 1000 ✓. DN200: 630/0.63=1000 ✓. DN600 c1: 6300/12.6=500 ✓. Good.\n\nShould accuracy_class for \"1 & 2\" rows be [1,2] or \"1 & 2\"? I'll use [1, 2] arrays. Hmm, in JSON rows, mixing int and array types in same column. The example rows for R137 didn't have such. I'll go with [1, 2]. Alternatively string \"1 & 2\". I'll pick [1, 2] for machine processing.\n\nNow — power supply column: since tables are named, I'll keep name conveying it. But to be safe, add \"power_supply\" column to each row? That makes rows self-contained. I'll add it: \"power_supply\": \"AC mains\" / \"Battery / Renewable energy\". Adds tokens but improves structure. Hmm... 50 rows × ~4 tokens. OK, I'll add it — actually no wait, let me keep tables as-is and rely on name/description. The schema example didn't add extra columns. Keep as-is. Hmm, I'll add description explicitly stating power supply.\n\nFootnotes global:\n[{\"marker\": \"1\", \"text\": \"The software version and checksum are displayed on the meter during the circular sequence every 10 minutes. The software version and checksum are displayed sequentially with first for the Measurement part then the Communications part.\"}]\n\nAlso there's the \"---\" and page headers — ignore.\n\nRecommendation:\n{\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]}\n\nThe scheme \"A\" — from cert number. Include in both certificate and recommendation? The recommendation schema has scheme field. Include in certificate for sure; recommendation too (it's part of cert number pattern). OK.\n\nNow, is including \"scheme\": \"A\" fabrication? The number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" literally contains \"-A-\". OIML-CS cert numbers: for the Basic System the format is \"Rxx/yyyy-IL-cc.nn\" hmm. Let me recall: OIML-CS Basic certificates like \"R137/2016-NL1-19.01\"? And MAA/Scheme A? Actually, the OIML certificate format historically: \"R60/2000-FR-98.99\" (old) then \"R60/2000-FR1-14.01\". The \"A\" inserted: I've seen certificates like \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — hmm, actually I recall NMi certificates for water meters R49 with \"-A-\" meaning it's issued under the \"OIML-CS Scheme A\"? Hmm, no wait. Let me think. OIML-CS has two schemes: Basic and... The MAA (Mutual Acceptance Arrangement) uses \"MAA\" prefix? Certificates under MAA are like \"MAA R46/2015-...\"? Hmm.\n\nActually I recall: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — the \"A\" here might denote... Hmm. Looking at real NMi OIML certs: e.g. \"OIML Certificate Number: R49/2013-NL1-20.01\". And I've definitely seen \"-A-\" in some, e.g. \"R126/2012-A-DK-14.03\"? Hmm, I genuinely recall Scheme A certificates having the format \"Rxx/yyyy-A-IL-cc.nn\" where A = Scheme A (for the OIML-CS \"A\" scheme for modules under MAA). Actually yes! The OIML-CS (Certificate System) introduced in 2013-ish has \"Basic system\" and \"Scheme A\" (with MAA participation). Certificates under Scheme A include the letter \"A\" after the recommendation year: e.g., \"OIML R46/2013-A-DK-14.02\". Hmm, hmm. I'm fairly confident \"-A-\" indicates Scheme A in OIML-CS numbering. Given the schema explicitly asks for \"scheme (A|B)\", and the number contains A, I'll set \"A\".\n\nNow certified_type description: not present in fragment (no free text description). Omit description.\n\nAlso should I include the bullet notes as matrix description for both tables? Yes, included in description.\n\nAlso \"Please note that the flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as...\" — the description captures.\n\nLet me also reconsider model attributes: including \"power_supply\" options per model — all 15 sizes × both. And accuracy_classes [1,2] all. Including uniform attributes for 15 models adds ~15×3 lines. Maybe skip attributes and let tables convey. I'll include them anyway? Hmm — uniform info in 15 places is redundant. I'll skip attributes{} and keep models simple: {\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}. The tables carry the rest. OK.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: model_level q3/q4 — attribute labels: \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\" and \"overload_flow_rate_q4\". Canonical snake_case: could be \"q3_permanent_flow_rate\". The table columns \"Permanent Q3\". I'll use \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\". Fine. Unit \"m³/h\" — use Unicode ³ as in markdown. Yes.\n\nunit_id: for m³/h, unitsdb id is likely \"u:cubic_metre_per_hour\"? Not certain — rule says leave null unless certain. For type_level entries I'll include \"unit_id\": null explicitly (shape requires unit_id field? \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\" — includes unit_id). For type_level I'll include unit_id: null. For model_level attribute entries, the R137 example omitted unit_id at value level and used attribute-level unit_symbol. I'll add \"unit_id\": null at attribute level for completeness? The R137 example didn't. I'll match R137 example style: attribute-level {attribute, unit_symbol, values:[{model, value}]}. Maybe include unit_id: null at attribute level — harmless and more complete. I'll include.\n\nFor matrix_tables column_units — the R137 example had \"column_units\". Include.\n\nAccuracy class values in rows: 1, 2, [1,2].\n\nRatio column label: \"ratio_q3_q1\" → maybe \"q3_q1_ratio\". I'll use \"q3_q1_ratio\".\n\nColumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1_minimum\", \"q2_transitional\", \"q3_permanent\", \"q4_overload\", \"q3_q1_ratio\"]. \n\nNow the software tables: rows include remarks \"-\" → nu